  "notes": "Four documented eras — trance 1994-2007, electro/progressive pop crossover 2009-2013, big room and future house 2014-2019, tech-house and a trance return from 2020 — so the band spans the entry's own bracket, 124-142, centred on the crossover figure of 128. The drums are written as the union the entry describes: the trance-era device that survives into every later era is \"long snare rolls into transitions\", authored here with the widest ramp of the producer block, and the \"regular open hi-hat on the upbeat\" is why the open hat is near-unconditional on all four offbeats, while \"later eras use a bigger, more compressed kick with a hard clap and 16th hats\" supplies the loud accent tier and the 16th base. He documented the reason for the pivot himself — staying current alongside younger producers — which is why the model is one era-spanning set rather than a trance model: the vocal-feature form of the later eras is what the arrangement encodes, \"shortened radio form later (vocal verse → riser → instrumental drop)\". Bass moves from rolling 16ths to a sidechained saw to a rubbery filtered line; the encoded compromise is a near-root independent riff. ⚠ The research says the tempo bands and drum grammar come from the trance and future-house conventions; that mark is repeated here, and the velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
